freesurferformats Changes
=========================


Version 1.1.0 -- Track file readers, subsetting, streaming and writing
---------------------------------------------------------------------
- FIX: `read.dti.tck()` and `read.dti.tsf()` now support gzip-compressed files (`.tck.gz`, `.tsf.gz`). They used to return garbage (plausible-looking tracks with coordinates around +/-3.4e38) without any error, because the header was decompressed transparently but the payload was read from a raw connection, so compressed bytes were interpreted as coordinates. QSIRecon and MRtrix pipelines write `.tck.gz`, and the whole-brain tractogram of a single subject can easily exceed 8 GB. Compression is now detected from the gzip magic bytes rather than the file name, so renamed files work too.
- FIX: `read.dti.tck()` and `read.dti.tsf()` no longer derive the payload size from the file size, which is meaningless for compressed files. Reading now validates the header (first line, `END`, `datatype`, `file` entry, data offset) and stops at the end-of-file marker instead.
- FIX: Remove the 100 KB minimum file size heuristic in `read.dti.tck()`, which rejected valid small TCK/TSF files. A valid 130 byte file with 2 streamlines is now read.
- FIX: The TCK/TSF header is no longer limited to 50 lines. Real headers from e.g. QSIRecon contain a `command_history` entry of arbitrary length, and any header longer than 50 lines used to fail with 'invalid format'. Header values may now contain colons as well.
- FIX: The TCK/TSF `count` header entry is optional, as documented by MRtrix (only `file` and `datatype` are required). Files without it caused an 'argument is of length zero' error, and files with a count larger than 2^31-1 caused 'missing value where TRUE/FALSE needed'.
- FIX: `read.dti.trk()` now reads all tracks until the end of the file when the `n_count` header field is 0, which per the TRK spec means that the track count was not stored. It used to silently return zero tracks for such files, i.e., it lost all data without a warning. A stored count that is larger than the data present is now reported as a truncated file.
- FIX: The track data in `read.dti.trk()` was read point by point (3 or more separate `readBin()` calls per point). Each track record is now read with a single `readBin()` call and split afterwards, which makes reading about 10x faster.
- FIX: `get.dti.trk.endianness()` crashed with 'argument is of length zero' for files shorter than 1000 bytes instead of reporting that the file is not in TRK format.
- ENH: `read.dti.trk()` and `read.dti.tck()` accept a new `max_tracks` parameter to read only the first N tracks. This makes it possible to inspect a very large tractogram without holding all of it in memory.
- ENH: The tracks returned by `read.dti.trk()` and `read.dti.tck()` are now stored in a compact container of class `fs.tracts` instead of a plain list with one matrix per track. All coordinates are kept in a single matrix plus an integer vector with the number of points per track, which is the same layout that `nibabel` uses (its `ArraySequence` class) and that the TRX file format stores on disk. This removes the per-track overhead of roughly 100 bytes plus one allocation, which dominates both runtime and memory for the millions of tracks found in whole-brain tractograms. **This is an API change**: `tracks[[i]]`, `length(tracks)` and `lapply()` keep working as before, but `tracks` is no longer a plain `list`, so code that inspects the object itself (e.g. `names()` or `str()`) needs adjusting. Use `as.list()` to get the old representation, or the new accessors `fs.tracts.coords()` and `fs.tracts.lengths()` and the predicate `is.fs.tracts()`. Subsetting with `[` returns an `fs.tracts` object.
- FIX: `read.fs.volume.nii()` now returns the voxel data in the raw NIFTI file storage order by default. Its `...` parameters used to be passed on to `oro.nifti::readNIfTI()`, whose default is `reorient = TRUE`. That reorientation permutes and/or flips the voxel data, but does not update the `sform` header fields, so the returned `data` array did not match the geometry derived from these fields in the returned header (and thus not `mghheader.vox2ras()`). This silently yielded wrong results for all NIFTI files whose `sform` is not aligned with the RAS axes, e.g. `inst/extdata/vol27int.nii.gz` or scanner-space data in LAS orientation, and it made volumes with an oblique (rotated) `sform` unreadable, since `oro.nifti` aborted with the error "Transformation is not simple, cannot reorient!".
- NEW: `read.fs.volume.nii()` has an explicit `reorient` parameter now (default `FALSE`) instead of relying on `...`. Reorienting on read, and passing a `nifti` instance that was read with `reorient = TRUE`, are discouraged and now raise a warning.
- NEW: `read.fs.volume.nii()` returns a `vox2ras_matrix` entry in the header if the NIFTI file contains `sform` or `qform` geometry information, just like `read.fs.mgh()` does.

- NEW: `read.dti.tck.header()`, `read.dti.tsf.header()` and `read.dti.trk.header()` read only the file header and never touch the track data. Reading the header of a 9 GB tractogram takes about 2 milliseconds, so a file can be inspected (or rejected early) before any decision about reading tracks is made. The TCK/TSF header is returned as a list, and the TRK header includes the derived `vox2ras` and `vox2ras_corrected` matrices that `read.dti.trk()` computes with `shift_origin = TRUE`.
- NEW: `read.dti.tck()`, `read.dti.tsf()` and `read.dti.trk()` accept a new `skip_tracks` and `bbox` parameter. The bounding box is given as `c(xmin, xmax, ymin, ymax, zmin, zmax)` and keeps every track that has at least one point inside it, which is what is needed to extract a region of interest (e.g. one hemisphere, or a box around a lesion) from a whole-brain tractogram. Both filters are applied while reading, so tracks that are skipped are never held in memory. The `bbox` is interpreted in the coordinate system of the coordinates that the function returns, i.e. in RAS space when `coords = 'ras'` is used for TRK files. Note that the number of tracks inside a box cannot be known before reading, so for very large files `bbox` should be combined with a `max_tracks` value that is known to be safe.
- NEW: `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()`. The first two scan a track file (TCK, TSF or TRK) once, in chunks, and report the number of tracks and the exact bounding box of all streamline coordinates, without ever holding a track in memory. This makes it possible to check the `count` entry of the header of a huge file, which is often missing or wrong. The iterator hands out one track at a time via `itr$next.track()` (and `NULL` at the end), with a constant memory footprint: iterating over 10 million tracks uses the same few dozen MB as iterating over the first 100, since only one chunk is buffered. It supports `skip_tracks` and `bbox` as well, and the connection is closed automatically when the iterator is garbage collected, or explicitly via `itr$close()`. For TRK files the count scan uses the fixed record size to skip over the tracks, so it is fast even for large files.
- NEW: `write.dti.tck()` and `write.dti.trk()` write `fs.tracts` instances back to TCK and TRK files. This complements the readers, so a subset of a huge tractogram can now be cropped out and saved: `write.dti.tck(read.dti.tck(f, max_tracks = 2000)$tracks, 'crop.tck')`. Coordinates, per-track lengths, and the file header are preserved exactly, and gzip compression can be requested per file name or explicitly. For TRK files the per-point scalars, the per-track properties and the big endian variant are supported as well. `write.dti.trk()` needs the geometry for the header: pass the header of a file that was read, or the result of `dti.track.bbox()`, or write with `coords_space = 'native'`.
- NEW: The `fs.tracts` constructor is now exported, along with `as.fs.tracts()` which converts the legacy list-of-matrices representation (as returned by `read.dti.trk()` in version 1.0.x) into an `fs.tracts` object. Together with the writers above this allows constructing and saving tractograms from scratch.
- ENH: `read.dti.trk()` has a new `coords` parameter and can now return the track coordinates in RAS space (`coords = 'ras'`, the space the tractography was performed in) instead of the raw coordinates stored in the file. The transformation is the one used by `nibabel` (including the `voxel_order` reconciliation), not a naive application of the `vox2ras` matrix from the header: for the XTRACT example file from the issue tracker the two differ by half a voxel. The default (`coords = NULL`) keeps returning the stored coordinates, but now warns if the `vox2ras` matrix in the header is not the identity, since the coordinates are then not in RAS space. Pass `coords = 'native'` to get the old behavior without the warning.
- FIX: A missing `Inf` end-of-file marker in a TCK/TSF file is no longer an error. MRtrix itself accepts the end of the file as the end of the data (the `in.eof()` check in its reader), and a missing terminator is now only reported if the `count` entry of the header shows that tracks are missing. Writing a TCK file with `count: 0` (an empty tractogram) used to make the reader abort with 'does not contain any streamlines'; such files are now read as an empty result.
- FIX: A `vox_to_ras` matrix that is all zeros in a TRK file means that the transformation was not recorded. It is now treated as the identity matrix (with a warning), as `nibabel` does, instead of being applied as a degenerate matrix that collapsed all coordinates onto the origin.
- FIX: `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()` refused to open an uncompressed tract file whose payload exceeds the memory limit, e.g. a 9 GB tractogram, with 'Requested allocation of 19.45 GB ... exceeds the safety limit'. They stream through the file and never hold it in memory, so that check must not apply to them, and it is now only used for whole-file reads. The same applied to `read.dti.tck(..., max_tracks = N)`, which asks for a subset and could nevertheless be rejected because of the size of the whole file, so a huge file could not be read at all, not even the first track. The up-front check also opened the file before validating it, which left a file connection behind when it aborted.
- FIX: A TRK file that contains a track without any point (which the format allows) was read incorrectly: the reader did not consume the properties of such a track, lost the sync with the file and then reported garbage, and it did not count the track, so `read.dti.trk()` could report a perfectly valid file as truncated. Empty tracks are now returned (as `nibabel` does), their properties are read, and they are counted by `read.dti.trk()`, `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()` and `dti.track.iterator()`. Note that a `bbox` filter drops them, since none of their (zero) points can be inside a box. This also means that a TRK file written from an `fs.tracts` instance that contains empty tracks round-trips exactly, which `write.dti.trk()` was already writing.
- FIX: `write.dti.tck()` now warns when the tracks to write contain tracks without points. The TCK format cannot represent them unambiguously: they are written as a bare delimiter, which every reader drops again (this package and `nibabel` behave the same), so the file would silently read back with fewer tracks than it was written from. Use `write.dti.trk()` if empty tracks have to be preserved.
- FIX: The memory limit message reported small limits and sizes in GB, so that e.g. a 48 KB payload was reported as 'Requested allocation of 0.00 GB ... exceeds the safety limit of 0.00 GB'. Sizes below 1 GB are now reported in bytes, KB or MB.
- ENH: The TCK/TSF readers and the streaming helpers take an advanced `chunk_values` parameter, the number of payload values that are read per chunk. It does not change the result, only the peak memory usage and the I/O granularity, so it is rarely needed; the default of 4e6 values corresponds to about 32 MB. Lower it on a machine with very little free memory.
- ENH: The test suite now covers the code paths that the multi-GB tractograms of real pipelines depend on without shipping any large file: the same small file is read with a dozen different chunk sizes, the memory limit is lowered so that the check triggers on a small file, a sparse file that reports 4 GB while occupying a few KB of disk is used to test the size-dependent branch, and the header of a real 8.9 GB QSIRecon/MRtrix3 tractogram is embedded to test parsing a realistic header. See the new `tests/testthat/test-dti-large-file-handling.R`.
- NEW: read and write support for diffusion MRI gradient tables (b-vectors and b-values), in the FSL bvecs/bvals format (`read.dti.bvec()`, `read.dti.bval()`, `write.dti.bvec()` and `write.dti.bval()`) and in the MRtrix gradient table format (`read.dti.grad()` and `write.dti.grad()`). This closes the largest gap for diffusion MRI users: a NIFTI file together with its '.bvec'/'.bval' pair is the standard exchange format of the field, and the package could previously read the resulting tracks but not the encoding that makes them interpretable. The new entry point `read.dti.gradients()` reads and validates a table, optionally checking it against the number of volumes of the image it belongs to.
- ENH: the gradient table readers detect the on-disk layout automatically. Both the layout that the FSL tools write (3 lines holding the vector components, b-values in a single line) and the layout used by other tools (one volume per line, e.g. the HCP files, and the MRtrix format with 4 values per line) are handled, and the choice can be forced with the new `layout` parameter. This matters in practice: a real QSIPrep subject file has 3 component lines for the b-vectors but 72 lines of b-values, so an implementation that assumes the single-line b-values layout of the FSL tools reads the wrong number of volumes or fails. A b-vector file with exactly 3 lines and 3 columns is genuinely ambiguous, and the reference implementations disagree about it (MRtrix treats the lines as components, DIPY as volumes); it is read like MRtrix does, and a warning points out the ambiguity instead of guessing silently.
- NEW: `read.dti.gradients()` also locates the files it needs when given only one of them: a single b-vector file makes it search for the matching b-values file next to it (e.g. `sub-01_dwi.bvec` with `sub-01_dwi.bval`), and a file with 4 values per volume is read as an MRtrix gradient table. This is a convenience that also prevents a common class of silent errors, since using the gradient table of a different acquisition, or reading a transposed table, produces plausible looking but wrong results without any error from the tools that consume it.
- ENH: `read.dti.gradients()` validates the entries of the table and reports the cases that are silently wrong in other implementations. A volume with a missing b-value or a missing direction is an error unless it can be interpreted as a b=0 volume, which is then done (the same rule that MRtrix3 implements, and the only case in which values are replaced). Gradient vectors whose norm deviates from 1 are reported, because MRtrix3 rescales the b-value by the squared norm in that situation, as are volumes with a b-value above the b=0 threshold but no direction, and volumes with a direction but a negligible b-value. Apart from the missing values, the data is always returned as it was stored: the vectors are never renormalized and the b-values are never rescaled, because either would silently change the data.
- NEW: transformation matrices are now returned as instances of the new `fs.transform` class, which state what the numbers in the matrix mean: which kind of coordinates it maps between (`space_in` and `space_out`, one of 'voxel' for voxel indices, 'ras' or 'lps' for world coordinates), whether voxel indices are zero-based or one-based (`voxel_base`), and which volumes the transformation relates (`src` and `dst`, with the volume dimensions, voxel sizes and the voxel-to-RAS matrix when the file records them). `read.fs.transform()` and its per-format readers return this representation, `is.fs.transform()` checks it, `print()` reports it in a readable form, `summary()` returns its properties as a machine-readable list, and `invert.fs.transform()` computes the reverse mapping (the inverse is never stored). Previously the readers returned an unclassed list of a matrix and a type string, so nothing in the returned object stated whether the matrix operates on voxel indices or world coordinates, or in which direction it goes, and code that mixed the two silently produced wrong coordinates. The space is taken from the file where the format states it (an LTA header states whether its matrix is VOX2VOX or RAS2RAS) and is 'unknown' where the format does not, instead of being guessed.
- ENH: the transformation readers now interpret the FreeSurfer volume geometry ('xras', 'yras', 'zras' and 'cras' in the volume info section of an LTA file) in the convention that FreeSurfer itself uses, which was verified against the reference implementation rather than assumed: `cras` is the RAS coordinate of the *center* of the volume, not of its first voxel, and the voxel-to-RAS matrix is `Pxyz_0 = cras - Mdc_scaled * (dim/2)`, exactly as `mghheader.vox2ras()` computes it for MGH headers. Using `cras` as the translation column of the matrix is an error of half the field of view. FreeSurfer voxel coordinates are zero-based in all of this: `mri_info --vox2ras`, `mri_info --vox2ras-tkr` and the `cras` of a real `recon-all` subject's `talairach.lta` were used to check each of these statements (the scanner and tkregister matrices differ by exactly `cras`, which is what `mghheader.tkreg2scanner()` computes).
- FIX: `read.fs.transform.dat()` returned the `intensity` entry of a register.dat file as an integer, which truncated the value. FreeSurfer writes a floating point value (the file shipped with the package contains `0.15`), so the function used to return `0` for it. It is now returned as a double.
- FIX: `read.fs.transform.xfm()` and `read.fs.transform.lta()` used to return a list with a `NULL` matrix for a file that contains no linear transformation matrix (e.g. a non-linear xfm), and callers failed later with an unrelated error. They now report that no 4x4 matrix was found in the file.
- ENH: the tests for the transformation readers now cover the coordinate spaces, the voxel base and the volume descriptors. The voxel-to-RAS matrix that a volume descriptor derives from an LTA volume info section is checked against `mghheader.vox2ras()` on a real volume, so the two implementations cannot drift apart, and a test states the `cras` convention explicitly (voxel index `dim/2` maps to `cras`).
- NEW: `read.fs.transform.fslmat()` reads FSL/FLIRT matrix files, i.e. the 4x4 text matrices that `flirt` writes with `-omat`. This is the registration format of the FSL world, and no R package could read it before. The returned object states that such a matrix maps (zero-based) voxel coordinates to voxel coordinates. `write.fs.transform()` and `write.fs.transform.fslmat()` write the format back. A transformation is only written if the format can represent it: an FSL matrix cannot store which two images it relates, nor a matrix in world coordinates, so `write.fs.transform()` refuses such a transformation with a pointer to `transform.to.voxel()` instead of writing a file that silently means something else.
- ENH: `read.fs.transform(format = 'auto')` now identifies the format from the file content as well as the extension. This is required because the extension '.mat' is used by FSL for text matrices and by ANTs/ITK for binary transformations, which have nothing in common: the content decides, and an ITK/ANTs file is reported as an unsupported format by name instead of failing with a confusing parse error.
- NEW: `transform.to.world()` and `transform.to.voxel()` convert a transformation between voxel and world coordinates, which is what makes a transformation file usable for coordinates that are not voxel indices, e.g. the coordinates of a surface vertex or a peak coordinate from another study. Volumes are passed as `fs.volume` instances (as returned by `read.fs.volume()`), as `nifti` instances or as headers, see `volume.geometry()`.
- ENH: the world space of an FSL matrix is now implemented as FSL, MRtrix3 and FreeSurfer do it, and this is not the obvious composition of the two image headers. FSL works in a space in which the voxel axes have unit length and in which the first axis is flipped if the header transform of the image has a positive determinant (its 'radiological' convention). Composing the two voxel-to-RAS matrices of the headers instead is wrong by more than 100 mm on real data, i.e. by more than the width of a brain, and it is wrong in a way that produces plausible-looking coordinates rather than an error. The conversion was verified against two independent implementations, FreeSurfer 7.4.1 (`lta_convert --infsl ... --outlta ...`) and MRtrix3 3.0.8 (`transformconvert ... flirt_import ...`), on two real image pairs with different voxel sizes and different determinant signs and for six different matrices: MRtrix3 agrees to a relative difference of about 1e-15, and FreeSurfer to about 1e-7, which is its single precision arithmetic. The check can be repeated with `dev_tools/check_transform_conversion.R`, which skips the reference tools if they are not installed. The new `fsl.scaled.voxel.matrix()` documents the rule, and the descriptor of a converted transformation carries the frame 'fsl' so that a result in the FSL world space cannot be mistaken for one in the RAS space of the image headers.
- ENH: a transformation that was converted to world coordinates records the geometry it was converted with. It can therefore be converted back to voxel coordinates without passing the volumes again, and the round trip returns the original matrix exactly. This is checked in `dev_tools/check_transform_conversion.R` for every matrix and image pair, in addition to the two reference implementations.
- ENH: the test suite covers reading and writing FSL matrices, the format detection (including the ITK/ANTs cases), the FSL world space rule, the geometry extraction from `fs.volume`, `nifti` and header inputs, and the conversion round trips, in `tests/testthat/test-transform_conversion.R`.
- NEW: `write.fs.transform.lta()`, `write.fs.transform.dat()` and `write.fs.transform.xfm()` write transformations in the three FreeSurfer formats that the package could only read until now, and `write.fs.transform()` dispatches between all four transform formats of the package (LTA, register.dat, xfm and FSL matrix) based on the file extension. The package could not write a single transformation file before this, so a transformation computed in R, or one read from a file of another format, could not be handed to FreeSurfer, FSL or MRtrix3 without writing the file by hand.
- ENH: like the FSL matrix writer, the new writers refuse to write a transformation that the format cannot represent, instead of writing a file that silently means something else. An xfm stores three matrix rows only, so it can hold affine transformations between RAS spaces, but not one in the FSL world space (whose coordinates are not the RAS space of an image header); a tkregister register.dat file holds a matrix that maps the voxel coordinates of the movable volume to the tkregister RAS coordinates of the target, and nothing else; and an LTA declares in its header whether its matrix operates on voxel indices or on RAS coordinates, so the type written follows from the spaces of the transformation, and a `type` field that contradicts them is an error. Each of these error messages points to `transform.to.world()` or `transform.to.voxel()`, which is the conversion that makes the file writable.
- ENH: an LTA file written by the package records the geometry of both volumes it relates (dimensions, voxel sizes, direction vectors and the center that FreeSurfer stores there), reconstructed from the volume descriptors of the transformation. This makes the file self-contained: FreeSurfer can convert it to other spaces without being given the volumes again, and the volume info section of a file the package wrote is identical to that of the FreeSurfer file it was derived from. A transformation without geometry gets a `valid = 0` section, which is what FreeSurfer writes when the geometry is unknown.
- ENH: the writers write matrix values with 17 significant digits, the number required for an exact round trip of a double. Using 15 digits, as other tools do, lost up to a few units in the last place (about 1e-14 for the translations of a typical registration), which the tests now check with an exact comparison rather than a tolerance.
- ENH: `read.fs.transform.dat()` also returns the subject identifier and the in-plane and between-plane resolutions of the file, in the `subject`, `in_plane_resolution` and `between_plane_resolution` fields. A writer needs them to reproduce the file, and the tkregister format states them in its first three lines.
- ENH: the files written by this package are verified against FreeSurfer itself, not only against the readers of this package: `dev_tools/check_transform_conversion.R` writes an LTA, a register.dat and an xfm file, has FreeSurfer's `lta_convert` read both the file written by the package and the original file it was derived from, and requires that both convert to the same transformation. The difference is 0 for all three formats, i.e. FreeSurfer reads the files as stating exactly what the files they were derived from state. The FSL matrix that the package writes for the same LTA agrees with FreeSurfer's own `lta_convert --outfsl` to single precision. The script performs 45 checks in total.
- NEW: `read.fs.transform.itk()` reads the ITK text transform format, i.e. the `.tfm` and `.txt` files that 3D Slicer (which calls it the 'ITK Transform' format), ANTs (via `ConvertTransformFile`), SimpleITK and the BIDS derivatives of fMRIPrep/QSIPrep work with. `write.fs.transform.itk()` writes the format, and `read.fs.transform()`/`write.fs.transform()` detect it from the file extension and the file content. These files carry the registrations of the derivatives that the fMRI community works with, so a transformation that previously had to be parsed by hand, or handed to another program, is now an `fs.transform` like any other.
- NEW: `transform.to.ras()` and `transform.to.lps()`. ITK works in world coordinates that are left-posterior-superior, while the other formats of this package use right-anterior-superior coordinates. The two conventions differ in the sign of the first two axes, so converting a transformation between them needs no volume geometry, unlike the conversion between voxel and world coordinates.
- ENH: the ITK reader implements the parameter handling that ITK itself specifies: the linear part is stored row by row, and the `FixedParameters` entry is the center of rotation, so the matrix is `y = A(x - c) + t + c` with the center folded in (`ComputeOffset()` in ITK computes `offset = translation + center - matrix * center`). Ignoring the center is an error of tens of millimeters on real files. This was verified against MRtrix3's `transformconvert ... itk_import`, which implements the same rule: for a real ANTs file with a non-zero center of rotation the two agree to 1e-14. A test also uses a forward and an inverse fMRIPrep ITK file, which must invert each other.
- ENH: the ITK reader reads affine transformations only, and reports everything else by name instead of misreading it: the classes 'AffineTransform_float_3_3', 'AffineTransform_double_3_3', 'MatrixOffsetTransformBase_float_3_3' and 'MatrixOffsetTransformBase_double_3_3', which are the ones the pipelines above write and the ones MRtrix3 supports. Other classes (Euler angle, versor, spline, displacement field) and files that contain several transformations (an ITK composite, e.g. the 240 concatenated transformations that fMRIPrep writes for motion correction) are refused with an explanation, since interpreting them would be a guess without a reference implementation to check against.
- ENH: binary ITK and ANTs transforms are recognized from their content and reported as unsupported instead of failing with a parse error. The '.mat' extension is shared by FSL text matrices and ITK binary transforms, so the content decides which one a file is.
- ENH: two limitations of FreeSurfer's own ITK support were found while verifying the new reader, and are documented in the reader and the writer: its `lta_convert --initk` rejects the 'float' variant of the classes ('readITK: Transform type unknown!'), and it ignores the `FixedParameters` entry, i.e. the center of rotation, so it reads a file with a non-zero center differently from ITK itself. (Verified by writing two files that encode the same transformation - one with the center in the fixed parameters, one with it folded into the translation - and letting FreeSurfer convert both: for the ANTs example file it reads them as differing by 7.4 mm in the translation, with identical linear parts.) The files that this package writes are in the form that FreeSurfer reads correctly, and the check script verifies that FreeSurfer converts a file written by the package to exactly the same transformation as the file it was derived from.
- ENH: the test suite covers the ITK reader and writer, the parameter order, the center of rotation, the LPS/RAS conversion, and the reported refusals, in `tests/testthat/test-itk_transform.R`, with real ANTs and fMRIPrep transform files in `extra_test_data/transforms/`.
- FIX: the four transformation converters were not exported, so they could not be called by users, and their examples failed with "could not find function": roxygen2 registered them as S3 methods (`S3method(transform, to.ras)`) instead of exporting them, because their names had the form `generic.class` and `transform` is a standard generic. They are now called `transform2world()`, `transform2voxel()`, `transform2ras()` and `transform2lps()` (previously `transform.to.world()`, `transform.to.voxel()`, `transform.to.ras()` and `transform.to.lps()`) and are exported as plain functions. The new names follow the convention that this package already uses for conversions, see `mghheader.tkreg2scanner()`. This also removes a warning and two notes from `R CMD check`: for a function named `transform.something` there is no documentation style that passes, since as a method it would have to have the signature of the generic (`transform(_data, ...)`, which does not match `transform2ras(tf)`) and as a plain function it is reported as "S3 methods shown with full name". The functions are unreleased, so the old names are not kept as aliases.
- FIX: the internal helpers `all.finite.rows()` and `all.infinite.rows()` are now called `finite.rows()` and `infinite.rows()`. The same naming rule applies to them (`all` is one of the base functions that R CMD check treats as a generic), which is why their man pages, added in the previous release of the development version, produced a check note.
- ENH: `R CMD check` now runs without errors, warnings or notes (it reported `1 ERROR, 1 WARNING, 1 NOTE` before, see the two entries above and the "Rd line widths" note for the `\examples` sections of the transformation functions, whose long lines were wrapped).
- FIX: the transformation writers formatted the matrix values with exactly 17 significant digits. That is the number of digits that identifies a double uniquely, but only if the decimal conversion of the platform reads them back correctly, and that is not the case on every platform: on macOS (ARM64) the round trip of a value such as `-1e-7/7` lost one unit in the last place, so the values that `write.fs.transform()` wrote were not read back exactly (the test suite reported "objects equal but not identical" on macOS only, all other platforms passed). The formatter now verifies the round trip of every value with the decimal conversion of the platform it runs on and increases the number of digits until the value survives it, which makes the written files lossless on that platform, and it never writes more digits than the platform can read.
- FIX: `read.fs.surface.vtk()` could not read any VTK file that a VTK version of the last decade wrote, in either encoding. Files written by VTK 5.1 and later do not store the vertex indices of the cells as one array of vertex counts followed by the indices (as the old reader expected), but as two separate arrays that are introduced by the keywords `OFFSETS` and `CONNECTIVITY` and that name their own data type, so the two numbers of the section header line mean something different than they used to (`POLYGONS 96 384` in the old layout versus `POLYGONS 97 288` in the new one). Reading such a file with the old assumptions produced either an error or silently wrong faces. On top of that, the ASCII reader assumed that every line of the `POINTS` section holds exactly one coordinate, which is true for the hand-written files shipped with the package but not for files written by VTK, which puts three coordinates (9 values) on a line: reading a VTK ASCII file that was saved from Paraview (or from any other VTK based tool) failed with 'more columns than column names'. Both properties, the cell array layout and the number of values per line, are now detected from the file content, because the version number in the header does not reliably state the layout (`dev_tools/check_vtk_conversion.R` verifies that a file written by VTK 9.6.2 still uses the layout of VTK 4.2 when it is asked to, and that this package detects it).
- NEW: `read.fs.surface.vtk()` reads binary VTK files, i.e. the encoding that VTK, Paraview, 3D Slicer and ITK-SNAP write by default and that is the only practical option for real meshes: the ASCII encoding of a cortical surface is two orders of magnitude larger than the binary one. Binary VTK data is always big endian (the format has no means of storing the byte order, and VTK never writes anything else), and the coordinates are stored as 4 byte floats or 8 byte doubles, depending on what the writer chose; both are read. The reader is implemented from the file format specification and verified against VTK itself: for every test file the result is compared against a dump of what VTK reads from that same file, which is exact for all binary files and agrees to 1e-7 for ASCII files (which store single precision values with 6 significant digits). The comparison includes a binary file that was written by a VTK version of the 4.2 era, i.e. one that this package did not write.
- NEW: `read.fs.tracts.vtk()` reads streamlines (tracts) from VTK files, i.e. from `POLYDATA` datasets that store a `LINES` section. That is the format in which Paraview, TrackVis and DSI Studio export streamlines, so a tractogram can now be read without converting it through the command line tools of another package first. The result is an `fs.tracts` instance, the same container that `read.dti.tck()` and `read.dti.trk()` return, and `tracts[[i]]` gives the coordinates of a single streamline. Points that are not part of any streamline are dropped with a warning, since the `fs.tracts` container stores the streamlines without gaps.
- ENH: `write.fs.surface.vtk()` writes the VTK legacy format in all four combinations of the two cell array layouts and the two encodings, which the new parameters `version` (4.2, the default, or 5.1) and `binary` (default FALSE) select. The default output is the layout that every VTK version can read, and it is byte compatible with what the function wrote before, apart from the version number in the header (which used to claim VTK 1.0 for a file in the 4.2 layout). Binary output is what makes the VTK format usable for real meshes: for a cortical surface it is roughly 20 times smaller than the ASCII output, which in turn is 20 times smaller than the FreeSurfer ASCII surface format. Files written by this package are verified to be readable by VTK itself, in all four combinations.
- ENH: only the geometry of a VTK file is read. Attribute sections (`POINT_DATA`, `CELL_DATA`, `NORMALS`, `TEXTURE_COORDINATES`, `SCALARS`, ...) are skipped, which is safe because VTK writes the geometry sections before the attribute sections. Everything that is not geometry is reported by name rather than misread: a `TRIANGLE_STRIPS` section, a mesh whose polygons are not triangles, a point cloud that has no polygons, a dataset type other than `POLYDATA`, an unsupported data type (the types that this package cannot size unambiguously, e.g. `long` and `vtkidtype`, whose width depends on the platform or on how VTK was built, are reported instead of guessed), a truncated section, and a file that is not a VTK legacy file. Passing a streamline file to `read.fs.surface.vtk()` (or the other way round) points the user to the other function instead of failing with a message about missing polygons.
- ENH: gzip-compressed VTK files (`.vtk.gz`) can be read, in both encodings. Other formats of this package support this transparently, so a VTK file that was compressed for transfer is no longer the exception.
- ENH: the test suite covers the VTK reader and writer in `tests/testthat/test-vtk.R` (144 checks): reading the reference files written by VTK in both layouts and encodings and comparing them against the dump of what VTK reads from them, the detection of the layout from the content rather than from the version number, the coordinate wrapping of ASCII files, the refusals listed above, gzipped files, the round trips of the writer in all four combinations, and the byte patterns that identify the layout of a written file. The reference files are generated by VTK (through pyvista) with `dev_tools/generate_vtk_test_data.py`, together with the dumps; `dev_tools/check_vtk_conversion.R` cross-validates both directions against VTK, and reading a real surface that FreeSurfer's `mris_convert` wrote (20 checks, all passing). Note that no other tool available on this machine can serve as a reference for the binary encoding: MRtrix3 3.0.8 has no VTK support at all, nibabel 5.4.2 reads only TCK and TRK, meshio 5.3.5 refuses `POLYDATA` by name (it reads and writes unstructured grids only), Connectome Workbench has no VTK support, and ITK's `VTKPolyDataReader` documents that it cannot read binary VTK files.

- NEW: `write.dti.tsf()` writes per-point track scalar values to a file in MRtrix TSF format, the format that stores one value per point of a streamline (e.g. the fractional anisotropy, the distance along the track, or values sampled from an image at the point coordinates). The package could read TSF files since version 1.1.0 but not write them, so the values it computed from a tractogram, or the ones it read from a file and modified, could not be handed to MRtrix, which is where track scalar files are used (`tcksample`, `tsfdivide`, `tsfsmooth`, ...). The input can be an `fs.tracts` instance that carries a single column of scalars (as returned by `read.dti.trk()` for a file with one scalar, or constructed with `fs.tracts()`), the `scalars` entry of the result of `read.dti.tsf()` (so that a scalar file can be read, modified and written back), a list of numeric vectors (one per track), or one vector of all values together with the new `lengths` parameter. A TSF file stores no coordinates and no track boundaries, so the number of values per track has to be provided: a TSF file on its own is meaningless, and every reader has to read it together with the tractogram it belongs to.
- ENH: `write.dti.tsf()` writes the payload layout that MRtrix itself writes, which is *not* the layout of the TCK format: a NaN value terminates every track, including the last one, and there is no `Inf` terminator. This is not a detail, since MRtrix verifies the number of tracks of a track scalar file against the tractogram it is used with, and a file whose values are not delimited exactly like the tracks of that tractogram cannot be detected as broken by a reader that splits on the delimiters (it just yields values that belong to the wrong tracks). The layout was established by reading the TSF files that MRtrix 3.0.4 wrote with `tcksample` and by validating the files written by this package with `tsfvalidate`: for the real whole-brain tractogram of the package (100000 tracks, 4.7 million values), the payload of the file written by this package is byte-identical to the corresponding part of the file that MRtrix wrote, and `tsfvalidate` reports 'Track scalar file data checked OK' for it. The new `dev_tools/check_mrtrix_tsf.R` performs this and 7 further checks against MRtrix3 3.0.8 (`tsfinfo`, `tsfvalidate` and `tsfmult`, for all four supported data types), and skips them if the tools are not installed.
- ENH: `write.dti.tsf()` refuses to write a track scalar file whose values cannot be matched to the tracks of a tractogram file: an `fs.tracts` instance with more than one column of scalars is reported (a TSF file stores exactly one value per point), and an empty track raises a warning, since it cannot be represented in a way that keeps the values aligned with the tracks of the tractogram (the same restriction applies to `write.dti.tck()`; `write.dti.trk()` preserves empty tracks). Writing an empty track list is allowed and produces a file with `count: 0`, which `read.dti.tsf()` reads back as an empty result.
- ENH: the header builder of the TCK/TSF writers is now shared (`build.mrtrix.header()` and `build.mrtrix.header.stable()`, previously `build.tck.header()` and `build.tck.header.stable()`), and the chunked payload writer as well (`write.mrtrix.streamlines()`). Both formats store the same ASCII header and a payload of interleaved float values, they only differ in the identifier line, in the number of values per point and in the terminator, so a single implementation keeps them from drifting apart. The refactoring does not change the output of `write.dti.tck()`: the files written by it before and after the change are byte-identical for all four data types, for gzipped files, and for a round trip of a real tractogram subset.
- FIX: `write.dti.tck()` and `write.dti.tsf()` no longer store the `derived` entry of a header that was read from a file. That entry is not a property of the file but the description of the payload that `read.dti.tck.header()` and `read.dti.tsf.header()` computed, so writing it back (which the documented read-modify-write workflow of the writers does) used to add a meaningless `derived:` line to the file and to shadow the entry that the reader computes.
- NEW: `write.fs.surface.stl()` writes meshes in STL format, in both the binary and the ASCII variant (the `ascii` parameter, default binary). The STL format is the format of 3D printing, and it is also what most mesh manipulation and viewing software reads and writes, so a surface that this package read can now be handed to those tools. It complements `read.fs.surface.stl()`, which the package has had for a while: it is the only mesh format of the package that could be read but not written. The new `mesh.face.normals()` computes the per-triangle normal vectors that the format stores, from the geometry (a degenerate triangle has no plane, so a zero vector is written for it), and the file is written in 50 byte records for the binary variant, with the layout verified against VTK: for the same mesh, the records of the files written by VTK 9.6.2 and by this package differ only in the sign of a zero.
- FIX: `write.fs.surface()` with `format = 'auto'` wrote a FreeSurfer binary surface (in the triangle format) for a file name ending in `.stl`, without any warning, since the STL format was missing from its list of formats. It now writes an STL file, and the ASCII variant can be requested by the file extension `.stla`, as some tools use it (`.stl` and `.stlb` are the binary variant). The new `write.fs.surface.stl()` refuses quad meshes with a pointer to `faces.quad.to.tris()` and 0-based vertex indices, as the other writers do.
- ENH: the STL readers implemented the documented behavior of returning the face normals of the mesh in the `metadata` property, but they dropped them instead (the normals were parsed and then discarded). Both readers now return them, computed from the geometry rather than taken from the file, since real-world STL files regularly store zeros or unnormalized vectors there. The misleading note of `read.fs.surface.stl.ascii()` that rebuilding the indexed mesh 'requires the `misc3d` package' is gone: the mesh is rebuilt by this package itself, in `polygon.soup.to.indexed.mesh()`.
- ENH: `read.fs.surface()` can read files with the extensions `.stla` (ASCII) and `.stlb` (binary) as well, which are used for the two variants of the STL format. The content decides which variant a file is, as before.
- ENH: the STL writer and readers are cross-validated against three independent implementations in the new `dev_tools/check_stl_conversion.R` (12 checks): VTK (through pyvista) reads the binary and the ASCII files written by this package and reports the same triangles, coordinates and normal vectors, this package reads a file written by VTK, FreeSurfer's `mris_convert` reads both variants of an STL file written by this package for the `fsaverage5` left hemisphere (20480 triangles, identical to the original surface) and writes an STL file that this package reads as that same surface, and meshio reads both variants. The script skips the tools that are not installed.
- FIX: `read.dti.trk()`, `read.dti.trk.header()`, `get.dti.trk.endianness()`, `dti.track.count()`, `dti.track.bbox()`, `dti.track.iterator()` and `detect.dti.tract.format()` now support gzip-compressed TRK files (`.trk.gz`), which they previously rejected with 'File ... is not in TRK format'. The TCK/TSF readers have handled compression since version 1.1.0, the TRK reader could not, because it seeks to fixed byte offsets in the file (the header size at byte 996, the payload at byte 1000, and the payload of skipped tracks) and R cannot seek in a compressed connection. The existing `skip.connection.bytes()` helper is used for all of those, the header is now read as a whole instead of being seeked to, and the compression is detected from the gzip magic bytes rather than from the file name. TrackVis and MRtrix do not read compressed track files themselves, so this is a storage feature (whole-brain tractograms are large and are regularly compressed for archiving), but it also means that a `.trk.gz` file from a pipeline that compresses its intermediate results can be read.
- ENH: `write.dti.trk()` has a `gzip` parameter, like `write.dti.tck()`: the file is compressed when the file name ends in `.gz`, and the parameter can force it.
- ENH: the compressed TRK support is verified against a compression of a real-world tractogram and against nibabel in the new `dev_tools/check_trk_gzip.R` (8 checks): the XTRACT tractogram of the test data (20 tracks, 1464 points, MIT licensed, see the attribution file in `extra_test_data/tracts/`) is read from the compressed and from the uncompressed file with identical results, nibabel reads both files written by this package (and agrees with the RAS coordinates that this package computes, to the last digit), and each of the five code paths that read a TRK file is exercised on a compressed file. Note that nibabel can read compressed TRK files but cannot write them, so a compressed file is always produced by compressing an uncompressed one.
- ENH: the gzip support of the TRK reader cannot be tested with the files that MRtrix or TrackVis write, since neither writes compressed files, so the test suite compresses an uncompressed file byte by byte (which is what the `gzip` program does) and compares the result with the uncompressed file for every code path: the header reader, the endianness detection (including a big endian file), the track reader with `max_tracks`, `skip_tracks` and `bbox`, the track counter, the bounding box scanner and the streaming iterator. The test data is a real tractogram of the right superior thalamic radiation, computed with the XTRACT protocol, in `extra_test_data/tracts/STR_R.trk` together with its compressed copy.
- NEW: read support for NRRD volumes (`.nrrd` and `.nhdr`), via the new `read.fs.volume.nrrd()` and `read.nrrd.header()`, and through `read.fs.volume()`, which now dispatches on these two file extensions and accepts `format = 'nrrd'`. NRRD (Nearly Raw Raster Data, the format of the 'nrrd'/'teem' libraries) is what 3D Slicer writes for its volumes, what the DTIPrep/DR-BUDDI/DTI-TK diffusion pipelines of the ENIGMA consortium produce and what the ITK/VTK world reads, so a volume from these tools could previously not be opened at all: this package read NIFTI and MGH/MGZ. The reader covers the whole header of the format that concerns data access: the field keys are matched case-insensitively and independently of the separators (`space directions`, `space_directions` and `Space-Directions` are the same field, and `key:=value` is accepted next to `key: value`, which is the spelling that pynrrd writes for string fields and that other writers copy). All data types of the format are read (8, 16, 32 and 64 bit integers, signed and unsigned, and 32 and 64 bit floats, both endiannesses), in all four encodings (`raw`, `ascii`, `gzip`, `bzip2`), for gzipped whole files, for detached headers that name their data file (including a compressed one), and for the `data file: LIST` mode in which the data is spread over several files. `line skip` and `byte skip` are honored, including the special value `-1` that means 'the data ends where the file ends'. The geometry is returned as the same `vox2ras_matrix` that the NIFTI and MGH readers return, built from `space`, `space directions`, `space origin` and `spacings`, with the conversion of the left-posterior-superior world space of the format to the right-anterior-superior space of the other readers, and shear in the space directions is preserved instead of being approximated by a rotation and a spacing. Diffusion weighted data is not only readable but interpretable: `DWMRI_b-value`, the `DWMRI_gradient_XXXX` vectors and the `measurement frame` are returned in the `dwi` property of the header as a b-value, a gradient matrix and a frame, with the gradients ordered by their index. R has no 64 bit integer type, so the three types that do not fit into a native R vector (32 bit unsigned, 64 bit signed and 64 bit unsigned) are returned as doubles, and the reader warns if a value in the file exceeds 2^53 and thus cannot be represented exactly, instead of returning a wrong number silently.
- ENH: the NRRD reader is verified value by value against an independent implementation of the format. `dev_tools/check_nrrd_conversion.R` reads every fixture with pynrrd (the reference Python implementation) and compares the values, the `dimension`, `sizes`, `type`, `encoding`, `endian`, `space`, `space directions`, `space origin`, `spacings` and the diffusion fields, and it reads every fixture with SimpleITK (i.e. ITK itself, the library that defines the format's space information) and requires that the `vox2ras_matrix` of this package describes the same geometry: the matrix is compared against ITK's origin, spacing and direction cosines, which are independent of how this package parses the header. It performs 29 checks (25 fixtures against pynrrd and/or ITK, two structural checks for the variants that pynrrd cannot read, a consistency check that the 12 files holding the same volume with different encodings, skips and skip values read identically, and a coverage check that no fixture is left unchecked). The dumps it compares against are written by the generator of the fixtures, so they are reproducible and not hand-edited, and the geometry of a sheared basis, which ITK cannot express, is excluded from the ITK comparison explicitly rather than being compared against a value that cannot be derived from ITK.
- ENH: NRRD test data and the test suite. The fixtures are generated by `dev_tools/generate_nrrd_test_data.py`, which also writes the reference dumps for the checks above. The small ones (a 4x3x2 volume with the values 1 to 24 in every encoding, the detached header variants, a big endian int16 file, a 4D file without a direction for its 4th axis, a 4D diffusion file with 6 gradients and an oblique basis) are in `inst/extdata/nrrd` and thus part of the package, so the 126 new tests in `tests/testthat/test-read_nrrd.R` run everywhere, including on CRAN: they cover the header fields, the dimensions and values, the four encodings, the detached and compressed variants, the geometry of the oblique and the left-posterior-superior files (with the expected matrix stated explicitly, not derived from the header again), the 4D and diffusion cases, the dispatcher, and the error paths (a file that is not NRRD, a missing `sizes` field, an unsupported data type, an unsupported encoding, truncated data and a file that does not exist). The larger and more exotic fixtures (all ten data types, a sheared basis, line skips, and the multi-file LIST mode) live in `extra_test_data/nrrd`, which is excluded from the package, and the tests that need them skip gracefully when the data is not present.
- ENH: NRRD is not a new format in R, and this package is not the first to read it: the CRAN package `nat` provides `read.nrrd()` and `write.nrrd()` (and a detached header writer), and `RIA` has a reader. This was checked in the source of `nat` rather than assumed, so the comparison is: `nat` reads detached `.nhdr` files and the `data file: LIST` mode as well, and it supports the encodings `raw`, `gzip` and `text` (its name for `ascii`), but not `bzip2`, it has nothing for the diffusion fields (`DWMRI_b-value`, the `DWMRI_gradient_*` vectors) and it skips `measurement frame` as an unhandled field, and it computes no world matrix from the space fields. The reader added here is read-only on purpose, and it adds no dependency (it uses the base R connections `gzfile` and `bzfile`), which is not the case for `nat`, whose `Imports` list pulls in several packages.
- NEW: read and write support for the ANALYZE 7.5 image format (`.hdr` plus `.img`), via `read.analyze.header()`, `read.analyze.data()`, `read.fs.volume.analyze()`, `write.analyze()` and the header helpers `analyzeheader.template()` and `analyzeheader.for.data()`. The same two files are also the on-disk layout of the *two-file variant of NIFTI v1* (the `ni1` magic), which is what FSL, AFNI and 3D Slicer write, and this is supported by the same functions: the two variants share the 348 byte header, but not its field semantics, so a reader that treats one as the other reports plausible looking garbage. Which variant a pair is, is decided from the last 4 bytes of the header (the magic field of NIFTI, the `smin` field of ANALYZE, which is empty for ANALYZE files), and the volume reader handles both. In practice this closes a gap for data from FSL pipelines, from SPM (which wrote `.img`/`.hdr` before NIFTI existed), from old scanners and from 3D Slicer, and it makes this package able to read the 348 byte header that it has been *writing* since version 1.0.0 (the ANALYZE compatible part of the NIFTI header, see the `write.nifti1()` docs).
- ENH: the ANALYZE reader implements the fields of the ANALYZE 7.5 specification, and not the NIFTI overlay of the same bytes: `vox_units`, `cal_units`, `dim_un0`, `funused1`-`funused3`, `compressed`, `verified`, `orient`, `originator`, `generated`, `scannum`, `patient_id`, `exp_date`, `exp_time`, `hist_un0`, `views`, `vols_added`, `start_field`, `field_skip`, `omax`/`omin`/`smax`/`smin` are read (and written) in the ANALYZE meaning. Reading an ANALYZE file with `read.nifti1.header()` returns zeroes for the NIFTI fields (`intent_code`, `qform_code`, `sform_code`, `srow_*`), which looks harmless for a file written by nibabel but is arbitrary data in general, since those bytes are `orient`, `originator` and the other history fields in an ANALYZE file. All data types of the format are read, in both endiannesses, and 4D data (and the `dim` field with up to 7 dimensions) is supported. Gzipped pairs (`.hdr.gz` with `.img.gz`) are read and written like the other implementations of the format handle them, and a pair can be given to the readers by the name of *either* of its two files, or by its base name without an extension.
- ENH: the geometry of the two-file formats is reported in a way that does not invent information that is not in the file. A NIFTI v1 pair stores a proper affine, and `read.fs.volume.analyze()` returns the same `vox2ras_matrix` that the single file NIFTI reader computes (from the `sform` if it is present, otherwise from the `qform`, including the quaternion and the `qfac` sign convention), with a `vox2ras_source` field that names the field it came from. ANALYZE 7.5 stores no affine at all: it has no field for the direction of the voxel axes in world space, which is the format's most famous defect, and means that the left/right orientation of an ANALYZE image cannot be recovered by any reader. There are two sources of geometry for such a file, and both are supported. The first is the MATLAB sidecar file that SPM and FreeSurfer write next to the image (`<base>.mat`, see the next entry), which is real information from the file set and therefore used automatically. The second is the convention that SPM, FreeSurfer and the reference implementation of the format (nibabel) apply when there is no sidecar file: the `originator` field gives the position of the image origin, the voxel sizes give the axis lengths, and the axes are assumed to point to the left, the front and the top. That one is a heuristic that the file does not state and can be wrong by a mirror image, so it is only used on request, via the new `spm` parameter (which also applies the data scale factor that SPM stores in `funused1`). Without a sidecar file and without `spm = TRUE`, no matrix is reported at all: the voxel sizes (`pix_dim`) and the orientation code (`orient`) are returned as they are stored, and the caller can build a matrix with the convention that fits their data. The SPM values themselves are always available as separate header fields (`spm_origin` from the first 6 bytes of `originator`, `originator_bytes` for the unmodified bytes, `funused1`), and files that use them are reported with a warning that names the fields and points at `spm = TRUE`.
- NEW: `read.matlab.v4.matrix()` and the automatic handling of the MATLAB sidecar file of ANALYZE images. SPM and FreeSurfer store the transformation matrix of an ANALYZE image in a MATLAB data file named `<base>.mat` next to `<base>.img`, which is the only *reliable* geometry such a file can have, and the first implementation of the format (and its successor NIFTI) never defined a field for it. `read.fs.volume.analyze()` now reads that file whenever it is present and uses its matrix, with `vox2ras_source = 'mat sidecar'`. The sidecar is a MATLAB v4 file, the uncompressed format that MATLAB used before version 5: a 20 byte header with the type, the dimensions, the imaginary flag and the variable name, followed by the raw values in column major order. `read.matlab.v4.matrix()` reads such files with no new dependency (about 60 lines), supporting all numeric types of the format, several variables per file, and both byte orders (which the header does not state, so they are told apart by requiring that the declared variables describe exactly the size of the file). Three details matter and are handled: (1) the matrix in the file maps the *1-based* voxel indices of MATLAB, while this package uses 0-based indices, so the sum of the rows of the rotation part is added to the translation -- a shift of one voxel, i.e. of several millimeters on real data; (2) the `M` variable that FreeSurfer writes does *not* contain the flip of the first voxel axis, while the `mat` variable that nibabel writes does, so the flip is added for `M` (this is what nibabel does as well, and it is the difference between a correct image and a mirrored one); (3) MATLAB files of version 5 and newer, which current MATLAB versions write by default and which are compressed and have a completely different structure, cannot be read -- such a file is reported with a warning and otherwise ignored, so the volume data is still returned instead of the whole read failing. A sidecar file that contains no matrix (or a matrix that is not 4x4 or is singular) is reported in the same way. The sidecar handling is verified against nibabel for a file that was written by nibabel's own SPM class with a known affine, and for a file written by FreeSurfer from the `tiny.mgh` volume that comes with the package.
- NEW: `write.analyze()` writes ANALYZE 7.5 files, i.e. the two files `<base>.hdr` and `<base>.img`, with the data type and dimensions taken from a header that `analyzeheader.for.data()` computes from the data, or from a header the caller adapted. Characters are written as ISO-8859-1 bytes, which is the inverse of what the reader does, so the content of the fixed size text fields (`descrip`, `originator`, `patient_id`, ...) survives a read and write cycle byte for byte, including the files in which SPM stores 3 little endian integers in `originator`. Since the format cannot store a transformation matrix, the documentation states this clearly and points to the NIFTI pair writer for users who need to keep the geometry.
- NEW: `write.nifti1()` can write the two-file variant of NIFTI v1 (`magic = 'ni1'`): if the header asks for it, `<base>.hdr` and `<base>.img` are written instead of a single file, the `vox_offset` field is set to 0 (it is meaningless for pairs, and a wrong value is now corrected with a warning rather than silently written), and a file name that does not match the variant is refused with an explanation instead of writing a broken file. `ni1header.template()` and `ni1header.for.data()` gained a `pair` parameter to create such a header, and `nii1header.for.mgh()` (which converts an MGH/MGZ volume to a NIFTI v1 header) gained one too, so `write.fs.volume()` can now write a `.hdr`/`.img` pair. Note that `write.fs.volume()` writes a *NIFTI* pair for these file names, and not an ANALYZE file: the `fs.volume` instance it is given contains the geometry, which ANALYZE could not store, so writing ANALYZE from it silently would lose the geometry. Use `write.analyze()` if you need actual ANALYZE output.
- ENH: `read.fs.volume()` now dispatches to the new reader for the file extensions `.hdr`, `.img`, `.hdr.gz` and `.img.gz`, and accepts `format = 'analyze'`. As with the other formats, a file that is given by its base name without an extension works if the two files of the pair exist.
- FIX: a serious one, found while implementing the above. `read.nifti1.data()` (and thus `read.fs.morph.ni1()` and `read.fs.morph.nii()`) read *unsigned* 8 bit and 16 bit data as *signed* values, since R's `readBin()` defaults to `signed = TRUE` and the code did not override it. Every value above the signed maximum was silently turned into a negative number: a `uint8` value of 200 was returned as -56, a `uint16` value of 40000 as -25536. NIFTI files with the `uint8` data type are the most common case of this (any 8 bit mask or label volume, including the `brain.nii` file that FreeSurfer's `mri_convert` writes for `mri/brain.mgz`), and the existing tests did not catch it because the files they read had no values above the signed maximum. Reading now goes through a single shared helper (`read.nifti.values()`) that uses the signedness of the data type, and the 32 bit unsigned type, which R cannot represent as an integer at all, is converted by hand from the two's complement values that `readBin` returns. The same bug existed for the ANALYZE reader during development, which is how it was found.
- FIX: `read.nifti1.data()` on a two-file header (a `.hdr` file with the `ni1` magic) used to read the *header file itself* as raw voxel data, i.e. it returned 348 bytes of header fields as an image, with no error and without touching the `.img` file that holds the data. The function now reads the data from the paired `.img` file (and warns if the header states a data offset, which is meaningless for a pair), and it reports a clear error if it is given a two-file header that is *not* a NIFTI file (pointing at the ANALYZE reader) instead of returning the header bytes as data.
- ENH: a clear error message in `nii1header.for.mgh()` when the MGH header of the given `fs.volume` contains no voxel size information, which is the case for volumes whose header has `ras_good_flag = 0` (e.g. `inst/extdata/tiny.mgh`). The function used to fail with the unhelpful 'replacement has length zero' from an assignment deep inside it.
- ENH: the ANALYZE support is verified against independent implementations of the format. The fixtures are generated by the new `dev_tools/generate_analyze_test_data.py` (with nibabel), which also writes reference dumps of the values, the header fields and the geometry of every fixture, and `dev_tools/check_analyze_conversion.R` performs 22 checks: (1) every fixture is read by our reader and compared field by field and value by value against the nibabel dump, including the geometry, the SPM interpretation and the SPM scale factor, and including a file whose `bitpix` field contradicts its data type and a file that FreeSurfer wrote; (2) nibabel reads the files that this package *writes* (`write.analyze()`, `write.nifti1()` for a pair and for a compressed pair) and reports the same values, voxel sizes, data type and affine; (3) FreeSurfer's `mri_convert` reads an ANALYZE file written by this package and the data matches the volume that was written, and our reader reads the ANALYZE file that FreeSurfer wrote from the same volume. `dev_tools/nibabel_analyze_dump.py` is the small helper that lets the R script ask nibabel about an arbitrary file. Both tools are optional, the script reports the parts that it skipped.
- ENH: the test suite covers the new format in the new `tests/testthat/test-read_analyze.R` and `tests/testthat/test-write_analyze.R`: the ANALYZE header fields, the values of all ANALYZE data types (including the signed 16 bit type with negative values and a `uint16` file with values above 32767, which is where the unsigned bug above would show), 4D data, compressed pairs, all the ways to name a pair file, the two NIFTI pair geometry variants (an sform and a qform), the SPM fields and the warning that reports them, the error paths, the dispatchers, and round trips through `write.analyze()`, `write.nifti1()` and `write.fs.volume()`. The small fixtures are shipped in `inst/extdata/analyze` so that the tests run everywhere, the larger ones and all reference dumps are in `extra_test_data/analyze`, and the tests that need them skip gracefully.


Version 1.0.2 -- Improved CIFTI support and security
-----------------------------------------------------
- NEW: support reading CIFTI dlabel (surface parcellation) and dtseries (surface time series) files via the 'cifti' package: new functions `read.fs.parcellation.cifti()` and `read.fs.series.cifti()`. The shared surface-extraction logic of `read.fs.morph.cifti()` was refactored into internal helpers.
- ENH: keep additional CIFTI test data (dlabel, dtseries, ptseries) in `extra_test_data` (excluded from the package via `.Rbuildignore`, since it would exceed the 5 MB CRAN size limit), and add a dev script to fetch it.
- SEC: add configurable max memory allocation via R option or env var, and other sanity fixes
- NEW: support reading a brain atlas from a LUT and region info in a CSV file. also support writing to these files.
- FIX: Fix the RAS geometry written by `write.fs.mgh` when a `vox2ras` matrix with anisotropic voxels whose axes are not aligned with the coordinate axes is given. The direction cosines (`Mdc`) are now normalized per column, i.e., each voxel size is applied to its own column, instead of being misaligned, which previously corrupted the stored orientation. This is a rare special case.
- ENH: Add tests that cross-validate the FreeSurfer reference files `brain.mgz` (shipped in `inst/extdata`) and `brain.nii` (kept in `extra_test_data`, which is not part of the package due to the 5 MB size limit). The tests check that reading both files yields identical voxel data and geometry, and that a NIfTI header generated from the MGH volume matches the FreeSurfer NIfTI reference. They are skipped when `extra_test_data` is not available (CRAN, installed package) and run in git checkouts of the repository, including continuous integration.


Version 1.0.1 -- Nifti conversion support and fixes
---------------------------------------------------
- FIX: Fix missing null bytes (string termination markers) when writing string in fs annot files, issue #34.
- ENH: Add `shift_origin` parameter to `read.dti.trk()` to optionally compute the corrected vox2ras matrix (the TRK file stores an mm2ras matrix, not a true vox2ras). Fixes issue #35.
- ENH: Add support for converting MGH/MGZ volumes to NIFTI v1 format, issue #28. New functions: `nii1header.for.mgh()` to create a NIFTI v1 header from an MGH header, and `write.fs.volume()` to write `fs.volume` instances to MGH, MGZ, or NIFTI v1 files. Includes internal helpers `nifti.dtypebitpix.info.from.mgh.dtype()` and `m44_to_quaternion()`. New tests added.
- FIX: Fix potential crash in `read.dti.tsf()` and `read.dti.tck()` when a file header contains duplicate keys, causing a "factor level duplicated" error in `.read.dti.tcktsf`.
- ENH: The optional test data can now be downloaded from a GitHub release as an alternative source, with automatic fallback to the rcmd.org server if unavailable.
- FIX: Remove reference to non-existent `tracks.trk` file from the optional test data listing.
- SEC: Default to https when downloading optional data


